From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Goyvaerts <jonathan.goyvaerts@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 16:35:17 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 6/9] hk: do not walk the tiles past the render area

A render covers the whole attachment even when its render area is a small
rectangle of it. Draws stay inside the render area and the tiles past its
bottom-right corner keep their contents, so give the render only the
extent up to that corner: the GPU then never visits those tiles.

User interfaces render this way: in the Counter-Strike 2 main menu
(2560x1440) about 1600 render passes a second draw one element into a
full-screen target with a render area under a tenth of it. At that size
the per-pass cost is dominated by fixed overhead and the frame rate moves
within noise; the tile walk shows on larger attachments. On the M4 Pro a
pass with a 128x64 area on an 8192x8192 attachment takes 24 us instead of
125 us (37 against 180 us when the memory clock is low).

Secondary command buffers that continue a render pass do not know the
area; their streams keep the full extent.

tests/vulkan_render_area.c checks that pixels inside and outside small
areas, including areas on the right and bottom edges, end up right (with
a clamp to half the area it fails), and prints the 8192x8192 timing.
dEQP-VK render-area cases and a sample of the renderpass attachment cases
give the same results as before.

Signed-off-by: Jonathan Goyvaerts <jonathan.goyvaerts@gmail.com>
Assisted-by: LLM
(cherry picked from commit b2ddff3cd1ce9a8f91c81478006ff162dc49d6de)
